\subsection{The pipeline and its two variable stages}
\label{app:pipeline}

\begin{table}[h]
  \caption{The five pipeline stages and what fixes the two that vary. Stage 3 is fixed by the answer format: position inside a list exists only if the agent emitted a list. Stage 5 is fixed by the metric: set F1 admits a keep-count, a recall-only metric does not, a single-answer metric admits only an argmax. Both are fixed before any benchmark is run.}
  \label{tab:slots}
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{llll}
    \toprule
    Stage & QAMPARI & ASQA & TriviaQA \\
    \midrule
    1 pool to units       & entity strings   & sentence clusters  & terse answers      \\
    2 support pattern     & identical        & identical          & identical          \\
    \textbf{3 confidence signal} & list rank, omission & sentence position & self-consistency \\
    4 cross-fitted logistic & identical      & identical          & identical          \\
    \textbf{5 decision rule}     & per-question budget & swept threshold & argmax     \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Feature definitions and controls}
\label{app:features}

Every candidate $c$ in question $q$ is mapped to a fixed vector by one function, \texttt{build\_features} in \texttt{analysis/weighting\_v3.py}. With $n=8$ agents and the five QAMPARI sources of Table~\ref{tab:blocks} the vector has 115 entries. Write $S(c)$ for the set of asserting agents, $r_m(c)$ for the zero-based position of $c$ inside agent $m$'s own output, and $L_m(q)$ for the number of distinct normalised items $m$ emitted on $q$, floored at 1.

\begin{table}
  \caption{The 115-feature construction and the 92 the method uses. Continuous entries are divided by a constant (5, or 2 and 40 in \texttt{content}) so that a single ridge coefficient is sensible across blocks. The four excluded blocks are the ones whose value is not established by an ablation; \texttt{verb} in particular is excluded on the control below, not on principle.}
  \label{tab:blocks}
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{llrp{6.1cm}}
    \toprule
    Block & In LEAN & Dim. & Entries \\
    \midrule
    \texttt{base}        & yes & 10 & intercept; one indicator per agent for $m \in S(c)$; $|S(c)|/n$ \\
    \texttt{rank}        & yes & 14 & min, mean, max and s.d.\ of $\log(1+r_m)$ over $S(c)$; min and mean of $r_m/L_m$; then $\log(1+r_m)$ in a per-agent slot \\
    \texttt{rankbuckets} & yes & 56 & per-agent one-hot over the seven position bands \\
    \texttt{omit}        & yes & 11 & over the \emph{silent} agents: mean and max of $\log L_m$, and the share with $L_m \geq 20$; then $\log L_m$ in a per-agent slot \\
    \texttt{stack}       & yes &  1 & $\operatorname{logit}$ of the support-pattern probability, refitted on the training folds of each fold \\
    \midrule
    \texttt{verb}        & no  & 10 & over the \emph{asserting} agents: mean and min of $\log L_m$; then $\log L_m$ per agent \\
    \texttt{qctx}        & no  &  4 & $\log(1+|C(q)|)$; max support in $q$ over $n$; share of $C(q)$ with support $\geq 2$; this candidate's support percentile within $q$ \\
    \texttt{content}     & no  &  4 & $\log(1+\text{words})$; contains a digit; string occurs in the question; truncated length \\
    \texttt{qtype}       & no  &  5 & one-hot over the five QAMPARI sources \\
    \midrule
    \textbf{LEAN total}  &     & \textbf{92} & \\
    All features         &     & 115 & \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{The seven position bands.}
Precision falls fastest at the start of an output, so the bands are logarithmic rather than uniform: $b(r) = \min(\lfloor \log_2 (1+r) \rfloor, 6)$, giving $\{0\}$, $\{1,2\}$, $\{3\text{--}6\}$, $\{7\text{--}14\}$, $\{15\text{--}30\}$, $\{31\text{--}62\}$ and $\{\geq 63\}$. Each asserting agent contributes a one-hot over its own seven bands, so the scorer can learn that a first item from one agent and a first item from another are worth different amounts --- the only place model identity and position interact.

\paragraph{Why asserter verbosity is excluded.}
\texttt{omit} and \texttt{verb} are the same measurement, $\log L_m(q)$, applied to the silent and the asserting agents. Only the first is in LEAN, which needs justifying, because the natural objection is that both are just verbosity. Table~\ref{tab:rank} restricts to solo assertions --- removing the support confound, since every row has $|S(c)|=1$ --- and stratifies by the asserter's own list length. The position gradient appears only on long lists, and the control in that table's caption is the decisive one: $P(\mathrm{correct} \mid \text{first position})$ across the four length bands is $2.2$, $4.1$, $4.4$ and $4.3$ percent, which is flat. If the signal were verbosity, first-position precision would fall as lists grow; it does not. The effect is positional. Adding \texttt{verb} on top of position is worth $+0.01$, so it is dropped and length survives only inside \texttt{omit}, where a silent agent has no position and length is the only evidence there is.

\subsection{Per-agent statistics}
\label{app:agents}

Tables~\ref{tab:lengths} and~\ref{tab:asqa-lengths} give the per-agent picture on both benchmarks. Three spreads matter, and they are what Section~\ref{sec:rq3} turns into a regime prediction.

\emph{Quality} spans $3.1\times$ on QAMPARI, from $8.98$ to $27.40$ set F1. \emph{Volume} spans $14.7\times$ in mean list length, from $4.9$ to $72.2$ items; this is the spread omission strength grades, and it is why silence is informative rather than a single bit. \emph{Solo precision} --- the share correct among claims an agent asserts alone --- spans $26\times$, from $0.22\%$ for \texttt{gpt-5.4-nano} to $5.76\%$ for \texttt{grok-4.3}. That last spread is the identity channel's entire opportunity at $|S(c)|=1$, where $87\%$ of all claims sit.

Volume and quality are close to unrelated here: \texttt{Kimi-K2.5} is both the best agent and the most verbose, but \texttt{gpt-5.4-nano} is the weakest while emitting more than \texttt{gpt-5.4-mini} by an order of magnitude in the mean. An agent's silence is therefore not a proxy for its accuracy, which is why \texttt{omit} adds to \texttt{base} rather than duplicating it.

ASQA has none of this structure. All six agents write $2.9$ to $3.8$ sentences on average, a spread of $1.3\times$ against QAMPARI's $14.7\times$, because the prompt asks for a single paragraph of about 80 words and the agents comply. Omission strength has nothing to grade, and the position signal is absent at that length as well (Figure~\ref{fig:position}). This is the concrete form of the claim in Section~\ref{sec:rq3} that ASQA is the benchmark where our arm loses: the output structure our features read is not present.

