\subsection{Prompts}
\label{app:prompts}

One prompt per benchmark, used unchanged for every agent. Each agent is sampled once per question at temperature 0 with a 12,000-token generation budget, in a single user turn with no few-shot examples, no retrieval and no system message beyond the prompt itself. Every result in the paper is computed from that one set of generations.

QAMPARI:
\begin{verbatim}
List every correct answer to the question, one per line, as a bare
list. No numbering, no commentary, no explanation. If there are many,
list as many as you can. Output only the list.
\end{verbatim}

QUEST, which differs only in naming entities rather than answers:
\begin{verbatim}
List every entity that satisfies the query, one per line, as a bare
list of names. No numbering, no commentary, no explanation. If there
are many, list as many as you can. Output only the list.
\end{verbatim}

ASQA:
\begin{verbatim}
Answer the question. It may be ambiguous and have several valid
interpretations -- cover all of them. Write a single paragraph of
about 80 words. Do not ask for clarification.
\end{verbatim}

\paragraph{Parsing and normalisation.}
List parsing is deliberately forgiving, because agents ignore the formatting instruction often enough to matter: numbering, bullets and trailing commentary are stripped rather than treated as list items. Items are then lowercased, stripped of punctuation and articles, and whitespace-collapsed. One step must run first: \texttt{Cohere-command-a-plus} wraps its output in literal \verb+<|START_TEXT|>+ and \verb+<|END_TEXT|>+ tokens, and punctuation-stripping would otherwise turn those into the words \texttt{start text}, producing a candidate that can never match gold while padding the precision denominator of every arm equally. Removing them is the parsing fix of Section~\ref{sec:protocol}; results computed before it are marked where they appear.

\subsection{Hyperparameters}
\label{app:hyper}

Table~\ref{tab:hyper} gives every setting. Nothing here was tuned against test folds: weights, likelihood tables, calibration maps, ridge coefficients, blend coefficients and keep thresholds are all fitted on training folds only, and the two benchmarks differ in fold count only because each ASQA threshold evaluation costs a ROUGE-L pass.

\begin{table}
  \caption{All fitting and evaluation settings. The gradient-boosted rows describe the rejected ablation of Section~\ref{sec:negative}, not the method: boosting is retained in the released code as the evidence for rejecting it. Dashes mark settings that do not apply to that benchmark's arms.}
  \label{tab:hyper}
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{lll}
    \toprule
    Setting & QAMPARI & ASQA \\
    \midrule
    Questions / agents          & 777 / 8 & 400 / 6 \\
    Cross-fitting folds         & 5 & 2 (halves, swapped) \\
    Fold assignment             & shuffle at seed 0, then $i \bmod 5$ & shuffle at seed 0, then split \\
    \midrule
    Scorer                      & logistic, 92 features & logistic \\
    Ridge $\lambda$             & $1.0$ & $1.0$ \\
    Solver                      & \multicolumn{2}{l}{IRLS, 30 iterations, intercept unpenalised} \\
    Threshold grid              & 600 quantiles & 160 quantiles \\
    \midrule
    Budget objective            & $2S_k / (k + \hat{G}_q)$, Section~\ref{sec:slotB} & --- \\
    $\hat{G}$ estimator         & ridge, $\lambda = 1.0$ & --- \\
    \midrule
    GBM trees (max / kept)      & 300 / $[85, 45, 58, 61, 46]$ & 400 / $[26, 28]$ \\
    GBM depth, learning rate    & 5, $0.12$ & 4, $0.08$ \\
    GBM subsample, bins         & \multicolumn{2}{l}{$0.8$; 32 quantile bins per column, fitted on train rows only} \\
    Early stopping              & \multicolumn{2}{l}{held-out log loss on the last $15\%$ of training questions, patience 25} \\
    Blend                       & logistic on that same $15\%$ slice & --- \\
    \midrule
    Bootstrap                   & \multicolumn{2}{l}{10,000 paired resamples over questions, $95\%$ percentile interval} \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

The threshold grid deserves one note. A global threshold keeps a prefix of each question's ranked candidates, so one pass per question prices every threshold at once; the grid is then the quantiles of the distinct training-fold scores, and 600 is well past the point where a finer grid changes the selected threshold. The budget arms replace that global threshold entirely, choosing $k$ per question from the predicted gold count.

\subsection{Extended negative results}
\label{app:negative}

Roughly sixty aggregation rules were cross-fitted on QAMPARI under the protocol of Appendix~\ref{app:hyper}, and the survivors ported to ASQA. The whole sweep ran on cached generations, so no result here required an additional API call and every rule saw exactly the same outputs the reported arms saw. Table~\ref{tab:negative} lists the rules that did not work, each with the reading it supports. They are worth the space because each one closes off an approach that would otherwise look untried, and because several are predicted to fail by the ceiling construction of Section~\ref{sec:ceiling} rather than merely observed to fail.

Three groups are worth separating. \emph{Capped by the ceiling:} error-correlation weighting, pairwise co-assertion and rank agreement are all functions of the support pattern, so Section~\ref{sec:ceiling} bounds them before they are run --- and error-correlation weighting selects $\lambda = 0$ in all five folds, reaching the same conclusion from the data. \emph{Destroys cross-question comparability:} within-question $z$-normalisation and percentile normalisation both score worse than counting, because a global threshold needs scores to mean the same thing in different questions, and normalising within a question deliberately removes that. \emph{Mistakes the mechanism:} a learned per-question $k$ loses $3.71$, while a single constant $k$, cross-fitted, picks $k=8$ and gains $+0.20$ --- so the budget's value is that it varies per question, not that it keeps less.

\paragraph{Alias merging, and why it fails.}
Merging string-similar candidates before scoring should consolidate split support, and it does not, at any threshold (Table~\ref{tab:alias}). The mechanism is visible in the merge counts: bad merges outnumber good ones everywhere, and the ratio worsens as the threshold loosens. String-similar QAMPARI entities are usually genuinely different entities --- sequels, people sharing a surname --- and a false merge deletes a correct answer outright rather than costing precision. This independently explains the earlier loss from LLM-judged clustering, and the budget's fitted saturation exponent, $\beta = 1.10$, says the same thing from the other side: duplicate spellings of one gold answer are rare in this data, so there is little to consolidate.

\begin{table}
  \caption{Alias merging at six string-similarity thresholds, with good and bad merge counts audited by hand. No threshold helps, and the damage grows as merging loosens. Pre-parsing-fix sweep, pending regeneration.}
  \label{tab:alias}
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{rrrrr}
    \toprule
    $\tau$ & Candidates & Good / bad merges & LEAN + budget & vs.\ no merge \\
    \midrule
    $1.00$ & 110,801 & 0 / 0     & $31.74$ & (reference) \\
    $0.95$ & 108,706 & 0 / 1     & $31.72$ & $-0.02$ \\
    $0.90$ & 106,167 & 7 / 10    & $31.68$ & $-0.07$ \\
    $0.85$ & 102,765 & 7 / 40    & $31.50$ & $-0.24$ \\
    $0.80$ &  95,090 & 8 / 105   & $31.53$ & $-0.21$ \\
    $0.70$ &  82,227 & 14 / 413  & $30.44$ & $-1.30$ \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{A number that is not headroom.}
Keeping the best prefix of each question on the ordering the model already produces scores $39.90$, some $36\%$ above the method. That figure is the maximum of roughly $143$ noisy prefix scores per question and is therefore heavily selection-biased: it is what the best threshold would have earned had it been known in advance, not what any rule can earn. Every honest attempt to collect it fell far short --- a learned per-question $k$ reaches $25.64$, a constant $k$ reaches $29.41$, the plug-in budget collects $2.4$ of a nominal $10.5$, and three separate reformulations of the budget objective each moved the result by at most $0.18$. It should not be read as available headroom.

\begin{table}
  \caption{Aggregation rules that did not work, with the reading each supports. Several land at or below the Section~\ref{sec:ceiling} ceiling, as that construction predicts. Pre-fix sweep, pending regeneration.}
  \label{tab:negative}
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{llp{5.4cm}}
    \toprule
    Rule & vs.\ count & Reading \\
    \midrule
    error-correlation weighting   & $\lambda=0$ in 5/5 folds & a function of the support pattern, so Section~\ref{sec:ceiling} caps it \\
    pairwise co-assertion, 28 indicators & $+0.05$ & the same conclusion reached discriminatively \\
    rank agreement across agents  & $+0.07$ & agents agreeing on order adds nothing further \\
    question-domain weights       & $+0.00$ & the five question sources carry no weighting signal \\
    Borda / CombSUM / weighted Borda & $-0.42$ to $-0.67$ & below counting; only RRF survives \\
    within-question $z$-normalisation & $-0.94$ & destroys the cross-question information a global threshold needs \\
    within-question percentile norm.  & $-2.56$ & the same, more severely \\
    learned per-question $k$      & $-3.71$ & $k^*$ is close to unpredictable from question features \\
    single constant $k$           & $+0.20$ & the budget's value is adaptivity, not keeping less \\
    alias merging, six thresholds & $-0.02$ to $-1.30$ & bad merges outnumber good at every threshold \\
    isotonic calibration, global  & $-0.03$ & see Section~\ref{sec:calib} for why this is not the null it appears to be \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}


